Web Shell Upload via Content-Type Restriction Bypass

Platform: PortSwigger Web Security Academy

Level: Apprentice

Category: File Upload

Status: Solved

1. What is this Lab?

In this lab, I learned how to bypass a file upload restriction.

The website only allowed image files like JPG and PNG. But I found that the website trusted the Content-Type value sent by the browser.

2. Tools I Used

Burp Suite professional

PortSwigger Web Security Academy

3. What I Did

Logged in to the lab.

Opened the profile image upload option.

Captured the upload request using Burp Suite.

Checked the file name and Content-Type.

Changed the Content-Type to image/jpeg.

Sent the modified request.

The server accepted the PHP file.

Opened the uploaded file.

Retrieved the secret file.

Successfully solved the lab.

4. What I Learned

How file upload works.

How to use Burp Suite.

How to change a Content-Type header.

How file upload restrictions can be bypassed.

Why file upload security is important.

5. Security Impact

If a website allows dangerous files to be uploaded and executed, an attacker may:

Run PHP code on the server.

Read sensitive files.

Access important server data.

Damage or compromise the server.

6. How to Prevent This Vulnerability

Check file types on the server.

Allow only safe file extensions.

Check the actual file content.

Do not allow PHP execution in upload folders.

Store uploaded files in a safe location.

